.TH LOGANALYZER 1 "November 2026" "Custom Shell 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
loganalyzer \- parse large log files with mmap and report statistics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B loganalyzer
.BI \-f " file"
[
.BI \-p " pattern"
] [
.BI \-l " level"
] [
.BI \-t " n"
] [
.BI \-k " field"
] [
.B \-c
] [
.B \-v
] [
.B \-h
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B loganalyzer
maps a log file into memory with
.BR mmap (2)
and scans it in a single pass, without copying the file into a user-space
buffer. It reports the total number of lines and bytes, a count for each log
level, the most frequent client addresses or request paths, and the elapsed
scan time.
.PP
Because the file is mapped rather than read, pages the kernel already holds
in its page cache are placed directly into the address space of the process,
so no copy is made between kernel and user space. Line endings are located
with
.BR memchr (3)
and each line is processed in place, through a pointer into the mapping.
.PP
The input is expected to be in common log format, for example:
.PP
.RS
192.168.1.5 \- \- [12/Nov/2026:09:14:03] "GET /api/users HTTP/1.1" 200 1234 INFO
.RE
.PP
The address is taken as the text up to the first space, and the path as the
second token inside the quoted request.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Log file to analyze. Required. The file must be a regular file.
.TP
.BI \-p " pattern"
Count only lines containing the given substring.
.TP
.BI \-l " level"
Count only lines of the given level: ERROR, WARN, INFO or DEBUG. The
comparison is case insensitive.
.TP
.BI \-t " n"
Report the
.I n
most frequent keys. Must be a positive number. Defaults to 5.
.TP
.BI \-k " field"
Field to rank, either
.B ip
or
.BR path .
Defaults to
.BR ip .
.TP
.B \-c
Comparison mode. After the analysis, count the lines twice, once over the
mapping and once through ordinary
.BR read (2)
calls, and print both elapsed times. Both passes do the same work, so the
comparison is a fair one.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose. Print the mapped size, the system page size and the number of pages
mapped.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage summary and exit successfully.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, or the file could not be opened, stat'ed or mapped.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted by SIGINT. Partial statistics are printed before returning.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT
Sets a flag that the scan loop checks every 100000 lines. The scan stops at
the next check, partial statistics are printed, the mapping is released and
130 is returned. The previous SIGINT disposition is restored before
returning, so the enclosing shell keeps its own handling.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B loganalyzer \-f /var/log/access.log
Print the standard summary.
.TP
.B loganalyzer \-f big.log \-l ERROR \-t 10 \-k path
Show the ten paths that produced the most errors.
.TP
.B loganalyzer \-f big.log \-c \-v
Compare mapped and read-based scanning, with mapping details.
.SH BUGS
At most 8192 distinct keys are tracked when ranking, and keys are truncated
to 63 characters. Lines whose key does not fit in the table are still counted
in the totals, and their number is reported, but they are not ranked.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mmap (2),
.BR madvise (2),
.BR munmap (2),
.BR sigaction (2),
.BR memchr (3),
.BR getopt (3)
.SH AUTHOR
Yeasin Arafat Riyad, Group 6, Operating System I.
